(a) Activation of emergency operations plan. A declaration of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency shall automatically activate the local emergency operations plan and shall be authority for the deployment of personnel and use of any forces to which the plan applies and for use or distribution of any supplies, equipment, materials, and facilities assembled, stockpiled or arranged to be made available pursuant to the Georgia Emergency Management Act or any other laws applicable to emergencies or disasters.
(b) Contracts with local governments. In addition to the normal agreements embodied in the applicable local emergency operations plan for mutual emergency assistance, the city may contract with any state or federal agency, county, or other municipality for the administration of a local emergency response program.
(c) Request for assistance. Consistent with a declaration of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the city manager may request the state or federal government, or any agency thereof, the governor of the state or county, or other municipality to provide assistance, provided that the disaster or emergency is beyond the capacity of the city to meet adequately and such outside assistance is necessary to supplement local efforts to protect property, public health and safety, or to avert or lessen the threat of a disaster or conditions giving rise to an emergency.
(d) Location of city business. The affairs and business of the city may be conducted at places other than the regular or usual location, within or outside of the city, when it is not prudent, expedient or possible to conduct business at the regular location. The board of commissioners or the city manager may establish one or more temporary locations for the continuation of city business or may otherwise move, transfer or relocate city offices, personnel or departments as may be necessary under the circumstances of the emergency.
(e) Public works contracts. Upon declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the city may contract for public works without letting such contract out to the lowest, responsible bidder and without advertising and posting notification of such contract for four weeks; provided, however, that the emergency must be of such nature that immediate action is required and that the action is necessary for the protection of the public health, safety and welfare. Any public works contract entered into pursuant to this subsection shall be entered on the minutes of the city as soon as practical and the nature of the emergency described therein in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 36-91-22(e). Any E-Verify affidavit or other state required affidavit shall be obtained from any contractor if otherwise required by law.
(f) Purchasing. Upon declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the purchasing ordinances, regulations or policies may be suspended. City officials shall continue to seek to obtain the best prices during the state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency.
(g) Code enforcement. Upon declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the board of commissioners may temporarily suspend the enforcement of the ordinances of the city, or any portion thereof, where the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency is of such nature that immediate action outside the Official Code of Ordinances of the City of Jesup is required, such suspension is consistent with the protection of the public health, safety and welfare, and such suspension is not inconsistent with any federal or state statutes or regulations.
(h) Fees. Upon declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the city may temporarily reduce or suspend any permit fees, application fees or other rate structures as necessary to encourage the rebuilding of the areas impacted by the disaster or emergency. The term "fees" include fees or rates charged by the city for building permits, land disturbance permits, zoning applications, special land use permits, temporary land use permits and other fees relating to the reconstruction, repair and clean-up of areas impacted by the disaster or emergency. The term "fees" does not include fees collected by the city on behalf of the state or federal government or fees charged by the city pursuant to a state or federal statute or regulation.
(i) Temporary dwellings. Upon the declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the city or its designees may issue temporary mobile home, trailer, recreational vehicle or other temporary dwelling structures or parks in any zoning district, even though not otherwise permitted by development code, temporary permits while the primary dwelling is being repaired. The temporary permit shall not exceed six months in duration. Upon expiration of the temporary permit and/or extension, the temporary dwelling must be removed.
(j) Further actions by the city manager in the event of a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency.
Following a declaration of a state of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency and during the continuance of such state of emergency, the city manager is authorized to implement local emergency measures to protect life and property or to bring the conditions giving rise to such emergency under control as set out in this section 30-6(b) and the following subparts.
(1) The city manager and his or her official designees shall have the legal authority to exercise the powers and discharge the duties conferred by law, including the implementation of the applicable local emergency operations plan, coordination of the emergency responses of public and private agencies and organizations, coordination of recovery efforts with county, state and federal officials, and inspection of emergency or disaster sites.
(2) In responding to a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency and in conducting necessary and appropriate survey of the damages caused by the emergency, the city manager and his or her official designee is authorized to enter at a reasonable time upon any property, public or private, for the purpose of evaluating sites involved with emergency management functions to protect the health, safety, and welfare of the public. The city manager is authorized to execute a right-of-entry agreement or similar agreement to use property for these purposes on behalf of the city; however, any such document shall be later presented for ratification by the board of commissioners at its next official meeting.
(3) Additional emergency powers. The city manager shall have and may exercise for such period as the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency exists or continues, the following additional emergency powers:
a. Use, employ, dispatch, allocate and assign employees of the city to assist in the safety and preservation of life, limb and property of its citizenry;
b. Close streets and sidewalks, and delineate areas within the city wherein an emergency exists;
c. Impose emergency curfew regulations;
d. Close business establishments within the affected area that present the imminent risk of harm to persons or property or as otherwise necessary to protect human health;
e. Close any or all city-owned buildings and other facilities to the use of the general public;
f. Do all reasonable acts necessary and incidental to the preservation of life, limb and property of the citizenry of the city;
g. Take such actions as may be authorized or compelled under any federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, or by any emergency order or directive issued by the federal or state government in connection with such emergency within the city;
h. Direct and compel the evacuation of all or part of the population from any stricken or threatened area, for the preservation of life or other disaster mitigation, response or recovery;
i. Prescribe routes, modes of transportation and destinations in connection with evacuation;
j. Make provision for the availability and use of temporary emergency housing, emergency shelters and/or emergency medical shelters;
k. Transfer the direction, personnel or functions of any city departments and agencies or units thereof for the purpose of performing or facilitating emergency services;
l. Utilize all available resources of the city and subordinate agencies over which the city has budgetary control as reasonably necessary to cope with the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency;
m. Utilize public property when necessary to address the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency;
n. Suspend any law, code provision or regulation prescribing the procedures for conduct of city business, or the orders, rules or regulations of any city agency, if strict compliance with any ordinance, resolution, order, rule or regulation would in any way prevent, hinder or delay necessary action in coping with the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, provided that such suspension shall provide for the minimum deviation from the requirements under the circumstances and further provided that, when practicable, specialists shall be assigned to avoid adverse effects resulting from such suspension;
o. Provide benefits to citizens upon execution of an intergovernmental agreement for grants to meet disaster-related necessary expenses or serious needs of individuals or families adversely affected by a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency or disaster in cases where the individuals or families are unable to meet the expenses or needs from other means, provided that such grants are authorized only when matching state or federal funds are available for such purposes;
p. Perform and exercise such other functions, powers and duties as may be deemed necessary to promote and secure the safety and protection of the civilian population, including individuals with household pets and service animals prior to, during, and following a major disaster or federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency; and
q. Take such actions as may be authorized or compelled under any declaration of a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, or under any emergency order or directive issued by the federal or state government in connection with such emergency within the city.
(Ord. of 6-7-2022(3))

614:
(a) To preserve, protect or sustain the life, health, welfare or safety of persons, or their property, within a designated area under a declaration of federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, it shall be unlawful for any person to travel, loiter, wander or stroll in or upon the public streets, highways, roads, lanes, parks or other public grounds, public places, public buildings, places of amusement, eating places, vacant lots or any other place during a declared emergency between hours specified by the city manager until the curfew is lifted.
(b) To promote order, protect lives, minimize the potential for looting and other crimes, and facilitate recovery operations during a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, the city manager shall have discretion to impose reentry restrictions on certain areas. The city manager shall exercise such discretion in accordance with the applicable local emergency operations plan, which shall be followed during emergencies. The provisions of this section shall not apply to persons acting in the following capacities:
(1) Officially authorized and essential law enforcement personnel;
(2) Officially authorized and essential health care providers;
(3) Officially authorized and essential personnel of the city;
(4) Officially authorized National Guard or federal military personnel;
(5) Officially authorized and essential firefighters;
(6) Officially authorized and essential emergency response personnel;
(7) Officially authorized and essential personnel or volunteers working with or through an emergency management agency (EMA);
(8) Officially authorized and essential utility repair crews;
(9) Citizens seeking to restore order to their homes or businesses while on their own property or place of business; and
(10) Other authorized and essential persons as designated on a list compiled by the city manager.
(c) Enforceability. This section shall be enforced by officers of the law enforcement personnel approved to provide aid and assistance during the federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency. Nothing contained in this section shall prohibit a law enforcement officer from bringing other charges under state law.
(d) Effective date. This section shall become effective only upon the signing of a declaration of emergency under section 30-5(c)(2), stating this section is in effect.
(Ord. of 6-7-2022(3))

615:
(a) In accordance with O.C.G.A. § 38-3-56, before building, constructing, repairing, renovating or making improvements to any real property, including dwellings, homes, buildings, structures or fixtures within an area in the city designated in a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency or disaster, any person, firm, partnership, corporation or other entity must register with the city clerk and secure a building permit that is posted at the work site. Each day any such entity does business in the city without complying with this chapter constitutes a separate offense.
(b) The cost of registration fees in a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency or disaster is fixed at $50.00 per annum. Registration is nontransferable. The cost of the emergency building permit shall be equal to the cost for a building permit under existing regulations. The permit shall only be authorized for repairs.
(c) When registering, any person, partnership, corporation or other entity making application must, under oath, complete an application, providing the following information:
(1) Name of applicant;
(2) Permanent address and phone number of applicant;
(3) Applicant's Social Security number or federal Employer Identification number;
(4) If applicant is a corporation, the state and date of incorporation;
(5) Tag registration information for each vehicle to be used in the business;
(6) List of cities and/or counties where the applicant has conducted business within the past 12 months;
(7) Georgia sales tax number or authorization;
(8) Georgia business license number, if required;
(9) Copy of license from Secretary of State, if required;
(10) A signed and sworn affidavit verifying the applicant's legal presence in the United States as required by O.C.G.A. § 50-36-1; and
(11) At least one secure and verifiable document as defined in O.C.G.A. § 50-36-2.
(d) Effective date. This section shall become effective only upon the signing of a declaration of a federal-declared emergency, state-declared emergency or local-declared emergency, stating this section is in effect. Unless otherwise specified in the declaration of emergency or otherwise extended by the board of commissioners, the provisions of this section 30-8 shall remain in effect during the state of emergency and for a subsequent period of three months following the end of such emergency.
(Ord. of 6-7-2022(3))
